# Firefly III stack secrets (services VM) — core, importer, Postgres
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/firefly.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting — the decrypt-and-source path expands it
# otherwise.
#
# ⚠ ONE OF THESE MUST NEVER BE ROTATED once the stack has data. See APP_KEY.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firefly III core
#
# ⚠ APP_KEY — NEVER ROTATE. It encrypts the Passport keypair stored in the
# database, so rotating it invalidates EVERY personal access token, including
# the one the data importer holds.
#
# ⚠ It must be EXACTLY 32 CHARACTERS (or the string `base64:` followed by 32
# decoded bytes). Any other length fails with
#   "Auth driver [remote_user_guard] for guard [remote_user_guard] is not
#    defined."
# — the encrypter throws while config is being resolved and guard registration
# collapses downstream, so the error names the auth config and says nothing
# about the key. Write this down; it is an afternoon otherwise.
# Generate: head -c 24 /dev/urandom | base64   (24 raw bytes -> 32 chars)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
APP_KEY=changeme_exactly_32_characters!!

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The host-side cron timer (nixos/configuration.nix) hits
#   GET /api/v1/cron/$STATIC_CRON_TOKEN
# which takes no authentication — the token IS the credential. It must be
# EXACTLY 32 characters: Binder\CLIToken checks `32 === strlen(...)`, and at
# any other length the route falls through to per-user token handling and
# returns a 500 that says nothing useful.
#
# Without it, recurring transactions never fire, bills are never marked paid
# and auto-budgets never roll over — all silently.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 16
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
STATIC_CRON_TOKEN=changeme_exactly_32_hex_chars!!

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL. Both spellings hold the SAME value: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# initialises the role in the database container, DB_PASSWORD is what Laravel
# connects with.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
DB_PASSWORD=changeme_same_value_as_POSTGRES_PASSWORD

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data importer
#
# 🚨 THIS ONE CANNOT BE GENERATED. It is a Firefly III personal access token
# and there is no CLI and no API path to create one: the artisan command is
# gated to APP_ENV=testing, and the token endpoint needs an authenticated
# browser session. Everything else about this stack is headless; this is the
# single manual step.
#
#   1. Confirm the Authentik outpost is sending your EMAIL before you log in
#      the first time. Firefly's remote_user_guard does no validation —
#      whatever string arrives becomes the owner account's identity
#      PERMANENTLY, and correcting it later is a database edit.
#   2. Log in at https://firefly.svc.idanreed.com through Authentik.
#   3. /profile -> "OAuth" tab -> "Create new token". Scope: all.
#   4. Paste it here. It is long (a JWT); keep it on one line.
#
# ⚠ Do NOT set FIREFLY_III_CLIENT_ID anywhere: /oauth/authorize needs a
# StatefulGuard and RemoteUserGuard is not one, so the OAuth code flow fatals.
# Declared unfixable upstream. Personal access tokens are the only option.
#
# The stack runs fine with this empty — the importer's UI loads and simply
# cannot reach Firefly. It is not a boot requirement.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIREFLY_III_ACCESS_TOKEN=
